YAGI-UDA ANTENNA — 100 YEAR ANALYSIS
→ 621.3824 → Antenna Engineering

: 100 years ago, in 1926, the Yagi-Uda beam antenna
was invented. A century later it remains a
cornerstone of directional RF.
: Hidetsugu Yagi (1886-1976) with early prototype 
and Shintaro Uda (1896-1976)

HISTORY
  Inventors  : Shintaro Uda (primary experimenter)
               Hidetsugu Yagi (professor / co-author)
  Institution: Tohoku Imperial University, Sendai, Japan
  First paper: Yagi & Uda, "Projector of the Sharpest Beam of
               Electric Waves", Proc. Imp. Acad. Japan, 1926
  Patent     : Filed by Yagi 1925-12, granted 1926-08

ANTENNA TYPES / ELEMENTS
  Driven     : Active dipole or folded dipole, fed by transmission line
  Reflector  : One (rarely more) parasitic element, slightly longer
               than driven element, placed behind it
  Directors  : One or more parasitic elements, slightly shorter
               than driven element, placed in front

KEY VARIABLES (typical VHF/UHF values)
  Wavelength     : λ = c / f
  Driven length  : ~0.47 λ to 0.50 λ
  Reflector      : ~0.50 λ to 0.55 λ
  Director       : ~0.40 λ to 0.45 λ (progressive shortening)
  Spacing        : 0.15 λ to 0.25 λ between elements
  Boom length    : increases with number of directors
  Typical gain   : 7–9 dBi (3-el)  →  12–15 dBi (7–10 el)
  Front-to-back  : 15–25 dB
  Impedance      : ~20–30 Ω (simple dipole) or ~200–300 Ω (folded)

FUNDAMENTALS
  Operation      : Parasitic elements re-radiate with phase
                   shift set by length and spacing so fields
                   add in the forward direction and cancel
                   toward the rear.
  Polarization   : Linear (horizontal or vertical)
  Bandwidth      : Narrow to moderate; more directors =
                   higher gain but narrower bandwidth
  Construction   : Simple metal rods on a boom; no need for
                   a complex feed network

MODERN USE
  Amateur radio, TV receive antennas, radar, satellite
  communications, point-to-point links.
